      { "title": "Sgt. Fury", "issue": "6" }
    ]
  },
  "subtitle": "[ASSET: left | ApplicationBlank | width=12%] This month's VMA goes to <i>X-Men</i> #4, where the Angel gets one look at the Scarlet Witch and thinks:\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"<i>Wow!</i>  What a dish!!&nbsp; If <i>she's</i> an evil mutant, I want an <i>application blank!</i>\"]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You just <i>have</i> to laugh here...)]\n\n[ASSET: right | PowderingMyNose | width=10%] But we should at least give a shout-out to the Wasp in Avengers 4 ([LINK:1964:AVEN_4|AVEN #4|2027-04-25]), who claims she was off powdering her nose -- right in the middle of a battle, something she says \"any girl would do in a moment of crises [sic]!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Yeesh!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Not only is this gag-inducing, but letterer Artie Simek can't even spell \"crisis\" correctly!)]",
  "review_text": "This month featured nine graded stories, spread out over eight issues (once again, <i>Strange Tales</i> providing its normal two), yielding a final weighted average of [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=5.93], which was over a point and a half better than last month's <b>4.31</b>.\n\nMy real surprise was that it was actually this low, as [SPAN: severity=success; text=all of the full-length books came in at mid-grade (6.0) or better] <i>for the first time</i>.&nbsp; But the absolutely [SPAN: severity=error; text=dreadful] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Tales to Abolish]<i> </i>continued to drag down everything else, and the Human Torch/Doctor Strange pairing in [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Strange Tales]<i> </i>didn't do the line any favors this month either.&nbsp; All three of those stories acted like an anchor that weighed down the relative greatness of the others (with Thor and Iron Man at least staying afloat).\n\nBut the month was notable for <i>other</i> reasons, not the least of which was the sudden influx of new (or returning) characters.&nbsp; The return of Captain America and Doctor Octopus were plusses, of course, but it was the intriguing Brotherhood of Evil Mutants that caught my eye (and its four new members).&nbsp; As for the new Black Widow character, the jury is still out...\n\nNew faces certainly helped, but it wasn't merely a matter of adding characters.&nbsp; Marvel also seemed increasingly willing to develop the ones it already had.\n\nIt seemed clear almost from the start that this month had a [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=greater emphasis on characterization], in particular, the Avengers, X-Men, Spider-Man, and Fantastic Four offerings, all of which demonstrated how interesting characters or situations could supersede weaker plots that would not survive scrutiny when held under a magnifying glass.&nbsp; (I think I made this clear in my various reviews this month.)\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I'm probably not alone in my desire for strong characters, even if tightly-woven plots are sacrificed a bit.]\n\nAll four of those titles <i>delivered</i> this month, not necessarily in terms of plot, but in terms of what makes a story truly engaging.&nbsp; If readers can be drawn in by the <b>characters</b>, they're <i>far more willing</i> to overlook silly things, such as Reed getting sick at the very moment the Thing needs him with strategy against the Hulk.&nbsp; If this month hinted at anything, it's that other magazines not named <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> seem to be finding themselves.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=Even if it's only for this one month, I'll take it!]\n\n<u>And one more thing:</u>\n[BR] This month featured two books that heralded [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=spectacle] on the covers, which were <i>Fantastic Four</i> and <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i>, both of which had covers depicting a showdown of sorts.\n\nBut what made them <i>different</i> was the fact that:\n[HANG] * For the FF, the spectacle <b>was the whole point</b> (meaning that the entire book seemed focused on moving the plot forward to get to the Thing/Hulk \"Battle of the Century\").\n[HANG] * But for Spider-Man, his battle with Doc Ock felt very much <b>beside the point</b> (i.e. \"fluff\" around the <i>real</i> plot).\n\nWhat I found most interesting was that it was the [SPAN: severity=success; text=<b>characterization</b> that won the day for <i>both</i> books,] even though they approached the spectacle from entirely different angles.\n\nThis is my opinion, of course, [SPAN: severity=info; text=but the net takeaway is that we all learned more about the characters than we did before.]&nbsp; Ben Grimm pulled himself up, but Peter Parker strolled away in resigned solitude.\n\nLet's not forget about Quicksilver and the Scarlet Witch, who are fascinating as well!\n[BR] And <i>boy</i>, did the FF issue provide a masterclass in weaving its characters between issues 3 and 4 of the Avengers!\n\n<i>Sure</i>, there were also the \"phone it in\" issues this month ([SPAN: severity=error; text=as the Human Torch and Giant-Man entries proved]), and <b>make no mistake</b>:&nbsp; those certainly dragged down the overall average, but as long as I feel the group as a whole offers a reason to come back every month, I'm much more willing to overlook a bad issue here or there.\n\nThe reality was that the <i>overall</i> quality varied greatly, with the good seeming to be <i>quite</i> solid and the bad <i>really</i> spoiling it for the others.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I still walk away from this month mostly satisfied and eagerly look forward to what's next!]\n\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** SUMMARY_BEGIN ***\n*** THIS MONTH ***9 graded stories over 8 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 6.33 grade character issue    weight 8.5   ff        FF #25   1.00 8.0   aven      AVEN #4  1.00 7.5   asm       ASM #11  1.00 6.5   xmen      XMEN #4  1.00 4.5   iron      TOS #52  0.50 4.0   thor      JIM #102 0.50 3.5   strange   ST #119  0.33 1.8   giant     TTA #54  0.50 1.5   torch     ST #119  0.505.93*** LAST MONTH ***7 graded stories over 6 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 4.58 grade character issue    weight 9.4   asm       ASM #10  1.00 7.5   thor      JIM #101 0.50 3.0   strange   ST #118  0.33 2.5   giant     TTA #53  0.75 2.5   torch     ST #118  0.50 2.0   iron      TOS #51  0.50 1.5   ff        FF #24   1.004.31\n*** SUMMARY_END ***]",
  "letters_page": "[HANG0] * Introduction of Captain America (to the Silver Age audience), Scarlet Witch, Quicksilver, Mastermind, Toad (all as part of the new Brotherhood of Evil Mutants), El Toro, Rabble Rouser, and the Black Widow.\n[HANG0] * In addition, both Hela and Sif made their first appearances, even if only in a backup adventure.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(That's a surprisingly robust list of familiar faces that suddenly entered -- or <i>re-entered</i> -- Marvel's world in a single month.)]\n\n[HANG0] * The legendary return of Captain America had to top the monthly highlights.\n[HANG0] * But the Thing versus Hulk bout also generated enthusiasm from the readers.\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** INTRODUCTION_BEGIN ***\nBaron Zemo | villain | unnamed | Milestone: (first)\nHela | villain | Milestone: (first: , in Tales of Asgard)\nLady Sif | Milestone: (first: , in Tales of Asgard)\nNotes | first | villain | Rugga, in Tales of Asgard\nNotes | villain | first | El Toro\nMastermind | villain | Milestone: (first)\nQuicksilver | villain | Milestone: (first), (origin)\nScarlet Witch | villain | Milestone: (first), (origin)\nToad | villain | Milestone: (first)\nNotes | first | villain | Brotherhood of Evil Mutants\nNotes | first | villain | Blackie Gaxton\nNotes | first | death | Bennett Brant\nNotes | first | Spider-Tracer\nNotes | first | villain | Rabble Rouser (Dr. Weinberg)\nNotes | first | villain | Aggamon\nBlack Widow | villain | Milestone: (first)\n*** INTRODUCTION_END ***]\n\n<b>Roundup:</b>\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_BEGIN ***]\n[HANG0] * The Avengers, Sub-Mariner, Rick Jones, and one stranded alien all play second fiddle to Captain America's legendary return.\n[HANG0] * Thor honors his oath to help Zarrko rule the future, and Odin finally realizes the truth, but the journey proves more routine than the setup suggested.\n[HANG0] * Giant-Man races across Santo Rico to rescue the Wasp and expose El Toro's election fraud.&nbsp; Readers may be less successful in finding the plot.\n[HANG0] * Magneto acquires a country, a nuclear bomb, and the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, while the Scarlet Witch and Quicksilver quietly steal the issue.\n[HANG0] * Doc Ock returns, Betty's secret is revealed, and Peter's latest chance at happiness falls apart.\n[HANG0] * The Fantastic Four are intentionally sidelined to pave the way for the first extended Thing versus Hulk battle, but even in defeat, it's Ben's character that wins the day.\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch battles the Rabble Rouser's campaign of mass persuasion, but readers may find themselves fighting to stay interested.\n[HANG0] * Doctor Strange tracks two burglars into the Purple Dimension, confronts its overlord, and wins a mystical beam battle that never quite comes to life.\n[HANG0] * The Crimson Dynamo returns under false colors as \"Boris\" attacks Iron Man, an ultimate sacrifice is made, and the intriguing Black Widow leaves readers with more questions than answers.\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_END ***]",
  "ownership": "[HANG0] * We have yet <i>another</i> new character who's a real dare-devil!\n[HANG0] * Thor has to battle a couple of <i>new</i> immortals\n[HANG0] * Giant-Man and the Wasp face the Human Top again\n[HANG0] * The Avengers \"take over\" where the FF left off\n[HANG0] * The Torch runs into Iceman\n[HANG0] * Doctor Strange visits an eerie house\n[HANG0] * Iron Man hasn't heard the last from the Black Widow\n[HANG0] * And Spidey gets unmasked in public!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Oh my!]"
}